<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<odoo>

    <!--
        An instructor may read the classes she is down to teach.

        Without this she can read none of them, and the whole instructor
        portal is blank: the dashboard, the class list, the roster and the
        attendance buttons all search calendar.event as her, without sudo.

        Nothing else reaches her. The two rules Odoo ships give portal users
        the events they are ATTENDING (partner_ids) and internal users
        everything; our instructors are portal users, and a fitness class has
        no attendees at all - fitness_core's create() clears partner_ids on
        every one of them, on purpose, because the roster is fitness.booking
        and a calendar attendee on a class is a phantom. On the production
        database that is 959 classes with nought attendee rows between them.
        The students' rule is scoped to group_fitness_student, which an
        instructor deliberately does not hold.

        So this rule is the only thing that lets an instructor see her own
        timetable. It existed on production as a record made by hand in
        Settings > Technical > Record Rules, with no xmlid and in no module,
        which meant every fresh install and every rebuilt database came up
        with the portal silently empty for staff. The migration alongside
        this file adopts that hand-made record so this definition updates it
        rather than creating a second one.

        Read only, and only her own: user_id is the Organizer, which is what
        every screen in fitness_teacher_swap already treats as "whose class
        this is".
    -->
    <record id="rule_fitness_teacher_calendar_event" model="ir.rule">
        <field name="name">Fitness Teachers: read own assigned classes</field>
        <field name="model_id" ref="calendar.model_calendar_event"/>
        <field name="domain_force">[('user_id', '=', user.id)]</field>
        <field name="groups" eval="[(4, ref('fitness_core.group_fitness_teacher'))]"/>
        <field name="perm_read" eval="True"/>
        <field name="perm_write" eval="False"/>
        <field name="perm_create" eval="False"/>
        <field name="perm_unlink" eval="False"/>
    </record>

</odoo>
